  "worked_solution": "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={x}^{y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)={x}^{y+h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}).\n$$",
